%n zizānam | i[nēru | nāʾe]ram | mupparšam | mubatt[iram]
#note: See @bib{RN117@270}: “The word @i{zi-za-na-am} is curiously written, half on the tablet’s left edge. There are two possible meanings, (a) a kind of grasshopper or locust, well attested, and (b) a half-share or measure known only from the lexical entry @i{MSL} XVI, p. 162, @i{Nabnītu} XVII 238: ½.tar = @i{zi-za-nu}. Which is appropriate here — if either — is uncertain.” The new parallel in SB V 308 suggests the reading adopted [EJ].
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 19
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 308

%n ... [...] ...
#note: See @bib{RN117@270}: “Perhaps @akk{ha-[ar-ha-ra-am ma-ṣa]-ru qi₂-iš-tim}, with OB Ishchali 30', but the rest of the line is obscure.”
// cf. L I.4 OB "Ischchali" 30'

%n mūšab (|) ilī | enunakkī || puzzuram | ipte
#note: See @bib{RN117@270}: “OB Ishchali is similar (37'–8': @akk{ir-ta-hi-<iṣ>} @i{qištam} / @i{mūšab Enunnakkī puzzuram ipte}), but what precedes and follows is different.”
// cf. L I.4 OB "Ischchali" 38'

%n šuwwiam | erēnam | šīham
#note: See @bib{RN117@271}: “Von Soden (‘die dir nun gehört’) and Hecker (‘deine’) took the second word as the accusative independent possessive pronoun, evidently reading @i{ku-wa-a-am}. However, the expected form in OB is @i{kâm}. Bottéro (‘un Cèdre extraordinairement élevé’) evidently parsed it as the accusative adjective @i{šūpâm} (cf. Tournay and Shaffer: @i{šu-pi-a-am}), but the uncontracted form would be @i{šūpuʾam} not @i{šūpiʾam}. I suppose it to be the verb @i{šeʾûm} in the imperative of the rare II/1 stem, with a glide in place of the glottal stop. This couplet exhibits an emphatic alliteration on /š/ lacking in the later version, which has @i{ibrī nittakis erēna šīha / ša muhhašu šamê nakpu} (SB V 293–4).”
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 314

%n lūpuš | daltam || ša qanâ | rupussa
#note: On the specific door described in these lines, and its counterpart in the Standard Babylonian version, see @bib{RN2651@12}.
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 316

%n ana bīti (|) ellil | libil (|) pur[a]ttum
#note: See @bib{RN117@271}: “As read the genitive construct state @i{bīti} either (a) retains its case-vowel, exhibiting the old-fashioned ending (as in OB II 165), or (b) exhibits crasis, @i{bīt-Illil}. One might instead read @i{qá-ti}, ‘to the hands of Enlil’.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 318

%n lihdū-ma | ummān | nippuri[m]
#note: See @bib{RN117@271}: “An alternative but less plausible reading is @akk{li-ih-du u₂-šu-um-gal NIBRU{ki}}, ‘let the dragon of Nippur become glad’.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 319
